Falsifies `pci::verification::read_bars_is_total_for_any_device`.

The defect: `read_bars` keeps every BAR it can size and drops the refusal of one whose base plus
size overflows. Every fixture's BAR sits far below the top of the address space, so nothing else
notices, but the kernel adds the two when it places, adopts and bounds a BAR.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert!(bar.base.checked_add(bar.size).is_some())`, on a 64-bit
BAR whose assigned base is within its size of `u64::MAX`, which is exactly the arbitrary-response
regime this harness quantifies over and nothing a fixture supplies.

diff --git a/crates/pci_configuration_space/src/lib.rs b/crates/pci_configuration_space/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/pci_configuration_space/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/pci_configuration_space/src/lib.rs
@@ -478,11 +478,7 @@ pub fn read_bars(
         // An unimplemented BAR is hardwired to zero and reads back zero, type bits included.
         // That is an empty slot, not a malformed one.
         if !(orig == 0 && mask_lo == 0) {
-            match size_from_mask(mask, decode_bits).and_then(|size| {
-                base.checked_add(size)
-                    .map(|_| size)
-                    .ok_or(BarRefusal::EndOverflows)
-            }) {
+            match size_from_mask(mask, decode_bits) {
                 Ok(size) => *slot = Some(Bar { base, size, is_64 }),
